<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dooars</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Duars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dooars are the alluvial plains along the southern foot of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, which control the passes into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>. Bhutan held them until the Duar War of 1864 to 1865, after which the Treaty of Sinchula ceded them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in return for an annual payment. They are now known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> gardens and wildlife reserves.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dooars.jpg" alt="Dooars" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rajibnandi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tea_garden_in_dooars.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Dooars lie south of the outer foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> and north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra River</a> basin, a belt about 30 km wide that stretches over about 350 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teesta-river">Teesta River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> to the Dhansiri River in Udalguri district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. They form part of the Terai-Duar savanna and grasslands ecoregion. The name means doors in several languages of the region, after the 18 passages between the hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> and the plains, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankosh-river">Sankosh River</a> divides them into Eastern and Western Dooars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Dooars belonged to the Kamata Kingdom under the Koch dynasty and carried the trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>: Assam sent muga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, endi cloth, dried fish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, and received woollen cloth, gold dust, rock salt, yak tails, musk, ponies and blankets. In the early 17th century the combined forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> and Koches checked the advance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutia">Bhutias</a>, who during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-mughal-conflicts">Ahom–Mughal conflicts</a> of the mid-17th century tried to extend their authority southwards.</p><p>Under the Ahom king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadhwaj-singha">Jayadhwaj Singha</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druk-desi">Druk Desi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> obtained the Darrang Duars by agreement in return for an annual tribute, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> holding them each year from June to October. In the last phase of the 18th century, while the Ahoms were occupied with internal rebellions, the Druk Desi took possession of them. Bhutan held the region when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> annexed it in 1865 after the Duar War, losing almost one fifth of its area. The western part became a district called Western Dooars, renamed Jalpaiguri district in 1869.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The region forms the northern bank of the Brahmaputra Valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and the plains of Kalimpong district, the whole of Jalpaiguri and Alipurduar districts and the upper part of Cooch Behar district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. Its towns include Cooch Behar, Alipurduar, Dhupguri, Malbazar and Birpara in West Bengal and Kokrajhar, Bongaigaon and Dhubri in Assam; Siliguri and Jalpaiguri, the largest cities, lie partly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a>. Its forests are home to endangered animals such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> tiger, the Indian rhinoceros and the Indian elephant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dooars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…attack in 1714, the British East India Company's ousting of the Bhutanese from Koch Bihar in 1772 and attack on Bhutan in 1774, a century of skirmishes and the Duar War (1864–65), after which the Treaty of Sinchula ceded the <b>Duars</b> to Britain for a rent of ₹50,000.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Civil wars among penlops weakened central authority, and Bhutan lost the <b>Duars</b> lowlands to the British in the war of 1864 to 1865.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhabdrung-bhutan">Zhabdrung Bhutan</a></li></ul></section>
